  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA shortwave trough was analyzed across Michigan, extending\nsouthward through Indiana into wrn KY. A lead shortwave and a\nlow level jet in southwest flow ahead of this trough was\nmaintaining areas of showers and a few thunderstorms early\nthis morning. This will continue to cross the region this\nmorning before exiting to the NE. Some initial decrease in\nshowers/storms is expected later this morning as a dry slot\nbriefly moves over the area. The main trough is expected to\ncontinue approaching the region this afternoon, along with\nassociated surface low pressure and a cold front. \n\nThe surface low is progged to track NE across OH, reaching NW \nPA by late afternoon/evening. A cold front trailing south, and a\nprefrontal trough ahead of it, should be the focus for\nadditional showers and thunderstorm development later today\nthrough early evening. HREF ML CAPE ranges from 1000-1500 j/kg\nby this afternoon, before diminishing after sunset. 0-6km shear\nis also expected to increase to between 30 and 40 kt across the\nregion with strengthening flow aloft, with some veering with\nheight also seen on the latest model soundings. The amount of\navailable instability will be affected by the amount of breaks \nin the cloud cover that can occur in the brief dry slot. If \nenough instability develops, then severe thunderstorms will be \npossible. Damaging wind would be the main hazard, though \nisolated tornadoes could also occur given the veering profile. \n\nPWATS are progged to increase to around 2.00 inches through the\nday, especially across far ern OH, nrn WV, and wrn PA. Issued a\nFlood Watch for flash flood potential today for this same area,\nas storms should be efficient rain producers. Models indicate\nsome potential for swaths of heavy rain, with probabilities for\n2 inches or greater highest in the flood watch area. Flash\nflooding will be possible, especially where any training occurs.\nStorm motion is expected to be parallel to the prefrontal \ntrough, and this could enhance the training potential especially\nacross nrn WV and wrn PA. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms should begin to diminish after sunset\nas instability wanes, with most of the activity east of the area\nby late evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe latest guidance has trended more progressive with the main\ntrough, keeping it an open wave and exiting the area later\nMonday. Maintained some POPs S-E of PIT especially on Monday, as\nthe trough completes its passage. Surface high pressure is\nexpected to build across the region later Monday into Tuesday,\nwith dry weather for most of the area. Ensembles then indicate a\nbroad trough should approach the region from the Central CONUS\nby midweek, as it phases with a low over the Tennessee Valley.\nThis trough is then expected to persist across the region into\nthe weekend. This pattern should result in daily, occasional \nshower and thunderstorm chances.",
